            0.531,
            0.116
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "(II) Preparing a soda-acid fire extinguisher"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.119,
            0.868,
            0.151
          ],
          "content": "**The reaction of acids with metal hydrogencarbonates is used in the fire extinguishers which produce carbon dioxide.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.155,
            0.848,
            0.172
          ],
          "content": "n Take 20 mL of sodium hydrogencarbonate $\\mathrm{(NaHCO_{3})}$ **solution in a wash-bottle.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.174,
            0.868,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": "Suspend an ignition tube containing dilute sulphuric acid in the wash-bottle **(Fig. 2.10).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.211,
            0.471,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "n **Close the mouth of the wash-bottle.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.23,
            0.868,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": "Tilt the wash-bottle so that the acid from the ignition tube mixes with the sodium **hydrogencarbonate solution below.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.266,
            0.6,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "n **You will notice discharge coming out of the nozzle.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.286,
            0.671,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": "Direct this discharge on a burning candle. What happens?"
        },
        {
